Two different logics put pramipexole and aripiprazole in the same patient, and they point in opposite directions.

*Logic 1 — the attenuation logic (ME/CFS framework).* The paper's full-vs-partial agonist analysis (@sec:full-partial-agonist-d2-d3) positions aripiprazole's low D2 intrinsic activity (~25–30% @Burris2002aripiprazoleD2) as a "cruise control" against pramipexole's "accelerator to the floor" in a D2-supersensitive system. Under this logic, adding low-dose aripiprazole (LDA, 0.25–2 mg) to a patient on pramipexole who is developing supersensitivity-type side effects (nausea, dizziness, sleep attacks at low doses) could cap the net D2 activation below the full-agonist ceiling. The cap is density-dependent: aripiprazole's intrinsic activity rises toward full agonism as D2 density increases @Tadori2011D2D3FunctionalPotency, so in the upregulated (supersensitive) state the cap degrades toward the full-agonist liability — the full qualification is the caveat in @spec:pramipexole-lda-combo-attenuation below.

*Logic 2 — the rescue logic (RLS evidence).* In severe RLS with dopamine-agonist augmentation, aripiprazole is used to *break* the full agonist's effect and enable its withdrawal: it is a counterweight that lets the full agonist be stopped, not a co-therapeutic that lets it be kept (@cf:pramipexole-lda-combo-evidence).

The pharmacological question both logics reduce to: does a partial agonist co-administered with a full agonist lower the net receptor activation below the full agonist alone? The in-vitro answer is *yes, with a density-dependent caveat that matters precisely for the supersensitive ME/CFS hypothesis* (below).

#speculation(title: [LDA May Attenuate Pramipexole's Peak D2 Drive — but Its Weakest Point Is the Supersensitive State It Is Meant to Protect])[
*Certainty: 0.25.* Adding LDA (0.25–1 mg) to pramipexole in an ME/CFS patient with low-dose side effects may reduce net D2 activation relative to pramipexole alone, because aripiprazole co-occupies a fraction of the same D2/D3 receptors — high affinity, low intrinsic activity @Burris2002aripiprazoleD2 — diluting the full agonist's maximal drive at the shared sites. That dilution is the in-vitro single-ligand answer; the co-administered two-ligand in vivo setting is where the attenuation is actually needed, and it is not directly measured (below).

*The Tadori 2011 caveat (mandatory with any restatement of this logic):* the partial-agonist buffering property is receptor-density-dependent. In recombinant human D2/D3 assays, aripiprazole is an antagonist at low D2L density, a low-intrinsic-activity partial agonist at low D2S density, a moderate partial agonist at D3, a *robust* partial agonist at high D2L density — and a *full* agonist at high D2S density @Tadori2011D2D3FunctionalPotency. The "attenuation" therefore holds in low-density (deficient) systems and degrades as receptor density rises. A putatively D2-supersensitive (upregulated, high effective density) ME/CFS system is precisely the state in which aripiprazole's stabilizing property is weakest and its net agonism strongest — so the attenuation hypothesis is least secure exactly where the ME/CFS framework needs it most. This does not refute the combination logic; it moves the prediction from "LDA is safer in supersensitive ME/CFS" to "LDA's effect in supersensitive ME/CFS is unpredictable in direction between attenuation and added agonist drive, and only a dose-response in the patient can resolve it."

*Competing explanations:* (a) In vivo supersensitivity in ME/CFS is not equivalent to in vitro high receptor density — upregulation may be functional (post-translational, coupling efficiency) rather than density, in which case the Tadori high-density branch does not apply and buffering is preserved. (b) The GarciaMalo-type rescue operates during a co-administration window (verified sequence: aripiprazole 5 mg/day initiated, followed by gradual DA tapering; the "dopaminergic stabilizing" reading is the authors' rationale, not adjudicated from the reported data @GarciaMalo2026AripiprazoleRLSAugmentation) rather than through steady-state efficacy dilution — the two logics would then describe different pharmacological events and should not be conflated. (c) The head-to-head TRD data (full agonist ≥ partial agonist, @Tundo2024PramAripTRD) show that in a population without a supersensitivity rationale, the full agonist simply works better — "attenuation" there is not a safety benefit but an efficacy loss, so any ME/CFS combination must be judged on the side-effect axis, not the efficacy axis. (d) The microglial-priming cascade is an independent generator of the supersensitive D2 state (@sec:microglial-cascade): primed microglia and local neuroinflammation upregulate receptor state without the chronic DA deficiency that the Tadori in-vitro branch was set for — Tadori set receptor densities by transfection in recombinant assays; it was never calibrated on any disease state, and the microglial-priming route to D2 upregulation is a hypothesis with no human-tissue evidence, labeled as such — and that cascade line itself invokes LDA as the tonic cap against supersensitive D2, which conflicts with this spec's own density caveat (the cap is weakest in exactly the upregulated state). The attenuation prediction is therefore least secure in the neuroinflammatory state where the combination is most often motivated, and only the within-patient dose-response this prediction specifies can adjudicate.

*Falsifiable prediction.* In ME/CFS (or RLS) patients on stable pramipexole with documented low-dose side effects, adding LDA 0.25–1 mg for 4–6 weeks should reduce the side-effect frequency (nausea/dizziness/sleep-attack diary) without worsening IRLS. Falsified if (i) side effects increase (added-agonist-drive branch — consistent with the Tadori high-density prediction), (ii) side effects are unchanged across the window with no net directional change (the attenuation is dose-flat — no dose window exists), or (iii) rescue, if it occurs, requires ≥5 mg aripiprazole (as in @GarciaMalo2026AripiprazoleRLSAugmentation) rather than the 0.25–2 mg window — in which case the "low-dose partial-agonist attenuation" framing is wrong and the drug is working as a higher-dose counterweight.

(Severity applicability: unknown at the evidence level — no data at any ME/CFS severity; the supersensitivity phenotype is not measured in any severity-stratified ME/CFS cohort. At the operational level the bounded experiment is not applicable to severe/bedbound patients: the side-effect diary, the IRLS control, and the supervised-contact bar are unmet at that severity — the same bar as the titration framework in @pwarn:pramipexole-mecfs-safety.)
(Origin: pharmacological inference from in-vitro D2/D3 functional pharmacology (@Tadori2011D2D3FunctionalPotency) + cross-disease RLS rescue data; no ME/CFS or fatigue-population combination data exist.)

*Consequence:* The "add low-dose aripiprazole to tame pramipexole" strategy is pharmacologically coherent but its predicted benefit is weakest in the exact patient state that motivates it; a patient considering the combination should do so as a bounded, monitored experiment (4–6 weeks, side-effect diary, IRLS control) rather than as an established safety measure, and should understand that the in-vitro data do not guarantee the protective direction in a supersensitive system, and that the 0.25–2 mg window is this paper's 2026 cap (certainty ≤0.35), not a derived efficacy range — the bounded experiment dose (0.25–1 mg) sits in the lower half of that window, and the cited RLS aripiprazole data are at 5 mg (the source's own "low-dose").
] <spec:pramipexole-lda-combo-attenuation>
